<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Data Statistics: ‣ 3.1 Datasets ‣ 3 Datasets, Models, Prompt Design and Metrics ‣ Enhancing the Learning Experience: Using Vision-Language Models to Generate Questions for Educational Videos" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the dataset structure and size statistics after our pre-processing steps.
The final dataset differs from the original version of the authors’ report <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> due to significant reductions:
In the TED-Ed dataset, no questions were provided for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>28</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28</annotation></semantics></math> videos, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="37" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>37</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">37</annotation></semantics></math> additional videos were inaccessible.
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7007" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7007</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7007</annotation></semantics></math> questions remained (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="72.09\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>72.09</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">72.09\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>), reducing the average question per video from <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.69" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.69</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.69</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.95</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the Khan Academy dataset, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="62" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>62</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">62</annotation></semantics></math> videos (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.82\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.82</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.82\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>) could not be downloaded, resulting in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="558" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>558</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">558</annotation></semantics></math> removed tasks (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99.73\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>99.73</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99.73\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> retained).
Video lengths vary considerably.
Khan Academy videos can exceed an hour, while TED-Ed videos are limited to around <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> minutes.
However, most videos are much shorter.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data Split:</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employed an <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> split for training, validation, and testing, randomly distributing video IDs across these sets (seed value: <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1234" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1234</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1234</annotation></semantics></math>).
Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Data Split: ‣ 3.1 Datasets ‣ 3 Datasets, Models, Prompt Design and Metrics ‣ Enhancing the Learning Experience: Using Vision-Language Models to Generate Questions for Educational Videos" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> summarizes the distribution of videos/questions across the splits.
Notably, the splits maintained an even balance between multiple-choice and open-ended questions, allowing for question-type-independent model training and evaluation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Pre-processed data statistics for train, val, and test sets. The first half represents the number of videos and the second half represents the number of questions.</figcaption>
