</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze question words generated by different models, averaging over all three prompts and considering <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wh-words</span> and <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how</span>.
Besides, we analyze sentence length (minimum, average, maximum) and readability (<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Flesch’s index</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>).
Like <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LearningQ</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, we use Flesch to compare readability across data.
This analysis aims to compare characteristics between generated questions and ground truth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.4 Evaluation of Question Structure ‣ 4 Comparing VLMs for Video Question Generation ‣ Enhancing the Learning Experience: Using Vision-Language Models to Generate Questions for Educational Videos" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> describes the percentage distribution of question words.
All models predominantly generate questions using <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">what</span>.
For fine-tuning, most ground-truth questions lack explicit question words, and when present, <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">what</span> is the most frequently used, influencing the learning process, and leading models to generate more "what" questions.
Zero-shot models’ behavior suggests that they learned this focus on tasks like question answering.
<span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PG-Video-LLaVA</span> shows significant variation, likely due to unpredictable behavior.
For <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video-LLaMA</span>, the distribution of all approaches is consistent.
In contrast, <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mistral-7B</span> has a broader variety, suggesting a brighter understanding.
Overall, a more balanced fine-tuning dataset might lead to a more varied distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 4.4 Evaluation of Question Structure ‣ 4 Comparing VLMs for Video Question Generation ‣ Enhancing the Learning Experience: Using Vision-Language Models to Generate Questions for Educational Videos" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> reports the results
on output length and readability.
For zero-shot, <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PG-Video-LLaVA</span> and <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video-LLaMA</span> generate significantly longer sentences on average than <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video-LLaVA</span>.
This also applies for <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mistral-7B</span>, where generated questions often include answers.
The models’ readability indicates that the text appears harder to read than the ground truth, while <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video-LLaVA</span> produces more readable outputs.
Fine-tuning improves readability and shortens outputs, as the training data consists of shorter questions.
Furthermore, a single word with "?" can serve as a question, which is reflected in fine-tuned models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Statistics of <span id="S4.T7.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mistral-7B</span>, <span id="S4.T7.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video-LLaVA</span> , <span id="S4.T7.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PG-Video-LLaVA</span> and <span id="S4.T7.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video-LLaMA</span> output across approaches (GT: ground truth. *: fine-tuned. <span id="S4.T7.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">V</span>: frames only. <span id="S4.T7.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A</span>: audio only).
Text length: <span id="S4.T7.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Min</span> stands for minimum, <span id="S4.T7.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Avg.</span> for average and <span id="S4.T7.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Max</span> for maximum. <span id="S4.T7.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Flesch</span> represents the score of Flesch’s index.</figcaption>
